ht  get out of the committee it might be  reported favorably or unfavorably and  then if it does it might be picked up by  the speaker and considered on the floor  and if it  might get a vote and in my pass and if  that happens it'll go on to the second  chamber so we break this whole process  down into four stages two in each of the  chambers and the first one is how likely  is the bill together the committee and  actually get to a floor vote and then  the second is if it gets to a four vote  how likely is it actually pass so can we  do this and it turns out we can actually  do this quite accurately and so for  every bill we provide a forecast of how  we think it's going to fare admin this  is a separate topic but we try to  visualize what the reasons behind it are  so transforming machine learning  features into human interpretable very  easily digestible output is something of  an interesting problem so we provide  that and then we try to provide how we  think every way just wait or is going to  vote on a certain issue so how do we do  this so we take all the data and all the  analysis that I talked about previously  all the legislative information the  relationships the external information  and then we try to put it together in a  host of different models to try to  predict at each stage on different  issues how we think things are going to  turn out so the reason we have all these  different models is because that  behavior is quite different at different  stages so here we have just a small  sample of the states with the percentage  of passage on the left and first we'll  start with the red which is how likely  is the bill to get a committee to a  floor vote and you see all of those are  quite low from five to ten percent to  about forty percent at the highest and  then the blue is assuming it did get out  of committee and got a floor vote how  likely is it actually to pass and that's  much higher so the main takeaways that  once things get to the floor they're  probably going to pass most of the time  over forty percent but the hard part the  bottleneck is getting that the committee  so that needs to be modeled very  carefully and so the distributions from  some of our models are here and you can  see that they're quite different some of  them have a long tail depending on the  stage there in the state some of the  more uniform a lot of things pass some  are sort of in between but there's a lot  of nuances that go on a lot of issues  that have to be taken to an account and  it's quite an interesting problem so the  main takeaways here are that legislative  information  although it might not be the first thing  to come to your mind is actually quite  crucial for decision making but for  people and for organizations and it's  hard to get it's very noisy is  unstructured it's not always very easily  accessible but once you have it there  are some really cool things you can do  with data analysis and start answering  some questions that might lead to really  interesting research problems which I've  touched on here a bit but I'd love to  talk more about if anyone's interested  thank you
 

